<h2 id=3D"Function:ManageSystemSettings-OktaGroups&amp;Scopes">Okta Groups =
&amp; Scopes</h2>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped relative-table confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 67=
.4249%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 51.547%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 48.4363%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Conversations</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Ref.</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <div class=3D"content-wrapper">
      <div class=3D"preformatted panel" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
       <div class=3D"preformattedContent panelContent">
        <pre>@Pierre, Joseph-Pascal 02-13 4:30 p.m.
Hi chief, tell me, when a CX user, connects to SOMA from CX, who gets the t=
oken SOMA needs to call its APIs?=20
1. CX
2. SOMA's SPA
3. Another component
Answer #2 was my understanding, but Marco thinks it is #1. The truth might =
be #3 which might be the adapter I recall you doing for loading a SOMA micr=
oapp (coded in React) within CX (coded in Angular) through an iframe.=20
=20
What say thou?

@Danda Da Silva Filho, Wagner 2025-02-14 9:21 a.m.
Its option 2 - SOMA goes to Okta and request the tokens. HOWEVER, Compass X=
 sends SOMA a list of scopes that the logged in user should have.

@Pierre, Joseph-Pascal 02-14 9:54 a.m.
Thank you Wagner! But where does CX retrieve the scopes and how does it sen=
d it to SOMA?

@Danda Da Silva Filho, Wagner 02-14 10:13 a.m.
CX retrieves the scopes from our own internal database
FUM
it sends via a browser session variable
</pre>
       </div>
      </div>
      <p><br></p>
     </div></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <div class=3D"content-wrapper">
      <p><br></p>
